To create two microservices using the above API gateway design pattern, you would need to modify the configuration class to define routes for each microservice. Here's an example:

```java
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class ApiGatewayConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator customRouteLocator(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route("microservice1_route", r -> r.path("/microservice1/**")
                        .uri("http://microservice1-host:port")) // Route to microservice 1
                .route("microservice2_route", r -> r.path("/microservice2/**")
                        .uri("http://microservice2-host:port")) // Route to microservice 2
                .build();
    }
}
```

In the above example, we define two routes. The first route routes requests with the path `/microservice1/**` to the `http://microservice1-host:port` endpoint, which represents your first microservice. Similarly, the second route routes requests with the path `/microservice2/**` to the `http://microservice2-host:port` endpoint, which represents your second microservice.

Replace `microservice1-host:port` and `microservice2-host:port` with the actual host and port of each microservice.

Ensure that you have the necessary dependencies and annotations in your main application class, similar to the previous example.

With these modifications, your API gateway will now forward requests to the appropriate microservices based on the defined routes. Requests starting with `/microservice1` will be directed to microservice 1, and requests starting with `/microservice2` will be directed to microservice 2.

