        <groupId>io.github.resilience4j</groupId>
        <artifactId>resilience4j-spring-boot2</artifactId>
        <version>1.7.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

Next, create a service that you want to protect with a circuit breaker. Here's an example of a simple service that makes an external API call:

```java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.client.RestTemplate;

@Service
public class ExternalApiService {
    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;

    public ExternalApiService() {
        this.restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
    }

    public String callExternalApi() {
        String url = "https://api.example.com/endpoint"; // Replace with your external API URL
        return restTemplate.getForObject(url, String.class);
    }
}
```

In the above example, we're using `RestTemplate` to make a GET request to an external API. You can replace the `url` variable with the actual URL of the external API you want to call.

Next, create a circuit breaker wrapper around the `ExternalApiService` using resilience4j:

```java
import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class CircuitBreakerService {
    private final ExternalApiService externalApiService;

    @Autowired
    public CircuitBreakerService(ExternalApiService externalApiService) {
        this.externalApiService = externalApiService;
    }

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "externalApi", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public String callExternalApiWithCircuitBreaker() {
        return externalApiService.callExternalApi();
    }

    public String fallback(Throwable throwable) {
        return "Fallback response"; // Provide a fallback response when the circuit is open
    }
}
```

In the above code, we use the `@CircuitBreaker` annotation from resilience4j to wrap the `callExternalApi()` method with a circuit breaker. The `name` parameter specifies the circuit breaker's unique name, and the `fallbackMethod` parameter specifies the fallback method to be executed when the circuit is open.

Finally, create a Spring Boot application class:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class CircuitBreakerApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(CircuitBreakerApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

That's it! You now have a basic implementation of the Circuit Breaker design pattern using Spring Boot and resilience4j. The `CircuitBreakerService` class acts as a wrapper around the `ExternalApiService`, and the circuit breaker handles failures and fallback responses when necessary.

You can now run the application, and when you call the `callExternalApiWithCircuitBreaker()` method, it will be protected by the circuit breaker. If the external API calls fail or exceed a defined failure threshold, the circuit breaker will open and start executing the fallback method instead.

