```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const StarRating = ({ ratingValue, max, editStarRating, onRatingSelected }) => {
  const [stars, setStars] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    updateStars();
  }, [ratingValue]);

  const updateStars = () => {
    const updatedStars = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < max; i++) {
      updatedStars.push({ filled: i < ratingValue });
    }
    setStars(updatedStars);
  };

  const handleStarClick = (index) => {
    if (!editStarRating) {
      return;
    }
    const selectedRating = index + 1;
    onRatingSelected(selectedRating);
  };

  return (
    <ul className="rating">
      {stars.map((star, index) => (
        <li
          key={index}
          className={star.filled ? 'filled' : ''}
          onClick={() => handleStarClick(index)}
        >
          &#x2605;
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
};

export default StarRating;
```

In this React component, we use the `useState` and `useEffect` hooks to manage the state and lifecycle of the component, similar to how the AngularJS directive works. The `updateStars` function is called whenever the `ratingValue` prop changes, and it updates the `stars` state accordingly.

The component renders a list of `<li>` elements, representing the stars. The `filled` class is applied to the stars based on whether their index is less than the `ratingValue`. When a star is clicked, the `handleStarClick` function is called, which checks if `editStarRating` is `true` and then calls the `onRatingSelected` prop with the selected rating.

Note that the template from the original AngularJS directive has been replaced with JSX syntax in the React component. You can style the stars and add any additional logic or functionality as per your requirements.

