Yes, the JavaScript Fetch API can be used to make POST requests and set headers. The Fetch API provides a simple and powerful way to make HTTP requests in modern browsers.

To make a POST request with Fetch, you can use the `fetch()` function and pass the URL and request options as parameters. Here's an example:

```javascript
const url = 'https://api.example.com/post';

const data = {
  username: 'john',
  password: 'secret'
};

const options = {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(data)
};

fetch(url, options)
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```

In this example, we define the URL of the API endpoint we want to send the POST request to. We also define the data object that we want to send in the request body. The options object includes the method set to `'POST'`, headers where we can specify the content type and any additional headers like authorization, and the body property where we convert the data object to a JSON string using `JSON.stringify()`.

The fetch function returns a Promise that resolves to the response from the server. We can then use `.then()` to handle the response, parse it as JSON, and work with the data. Any errors that occur during the request can be caught using `.catch()`.

Make sure to replace the URL, data, headers, and handle the response according to your specific use case.

Note that the Fetch API is supported in modern browsers, but if you need to support older browsers, you may need to use a polyfill or an alternative library like Axios.

