To achieve that in a JavaScript project using Mongoose, you can create builder functions or static methods in your Mongoose models to convert between models and responses. Here's a basic example:

```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

// Define your Mongoose schema
const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  email: String,
  age: Number
});

// Create a builder function or static method for converting model to response
userSchema.methods.toResponse = function() {
  return {
    name: this.name,
    email: this.email,
    age: this.age
  };
};

// Create a static method or builder function for converting response to model
userSchema.statics.fromResponse = function(response) {
  return new this({
    name: response.name,
    email: response.email,
    age: response.age
  });
};

// Create your Mongoose model
const User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);

// Example usage:
const userResponse = {
  name: 'John Doe',
  email: 'john@example.com',
  age: 30
};

// Convert response to model
const user = User.fromResponse(userResponse);

// Convert model to response
const userRes = user.toResponse();
console.log(userRes);
```

This way, you can easily convert between models and responses using builder pattern-like functions in your Mongoose models.

