```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function EditableLabel({ text }) {
  const [editing, setEditing] = useState(false);
  const [editedText, setEditedText] = useState(text);

  const handleLabelClick = () => {
    setEditing(true);
  };

  const handleTextareaChange = (e) => {
    setEditedText(e.target.value);
  };

  const handleTextareaBlur = () => {
    setEditing(false);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {editing ? (
        <textarea
          value={editedText}
          onChange={handleTextareaChange}
          onBlur={handleTextareaBlur}
        />
      ) : (
        <label onClick={handleLabelClick}>{editedText}</label>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export default EditableLabel;
```

In this component, `EditableLabel`, we're using the `useState` hook to manage the editing state and the edited text. When the label is clicked (`handleLabelClick` function), we switch to editing mode by setting the `editing` state to `true`. The label is replaced with a textarea where the user can edit the text. When the textarea loses focus (`handleTextareaBlur` function), we switch back to non-editing mode and update the `editedText`.

To use this component, you can simply import and include it in your main application file (e.g., `App.js`) like this:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import EditableLabel from './EditableLabel';

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Editable Label Example</h1>
      <EditableLabel text="Click to edit this label." />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

