Yes, you can store an `Employee` object without initially assigning it to a `Department`. To allow an `Employee` to have no associated `Department`, you can modify the `@ManyToOne` association in the `Employee` class to be optional by using the `optional = true` attribute of the `@JoinColumn` annotation.

Here's an updated version of the `Employee` class with an optional association to `Department`:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "employees")
class Employee {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @ManyToOne(optional = true)
    @JoinColumn(name = "department_id")
    private Department department;

    // ...

    // Getters and setters
}
```

By setting `optional = true`, Hibernate will allow the `Employee` object to be persisted even if the `department` field is `null`.

Here's an example of storing an `Employee` object without initially assigning it to a `Department`:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new employee without a department
        Employee employee = new Employee();
        employee.setName("John Doe");

        // Save the employee
        try (Session session = HibernateUtil.getSessionFactory().openSession()) {
            Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();
            session.save(employee);
            transaction.commit();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Retrieve the employee and check if the department is null
        try (Session session = HibernateUtil.getSessionFactory().openSession()) {
            Employee retrievedEmployee = session.get(Employee.class, employee.getId());
            System.out.println("Employee Name: " + retrievedEmployee.getName());
            System.out.println("Department: " + retrievedEmployee.getDepartment()); // Will print null
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we create an `Employee` object named "John Doe" without assigning it to a `Department`. We then save the `Employee` using a Hibernate session. When we retrieve the `Employee` from the database, the `department` field will be `null` since we didn't set any `Department` for the employee.

This way, you can store an `Employee` without initially associating it with a `Department`, making the association optional.

