In the `Employee` class, the `department` field is annotated with `@ManyToOne` to establish a many-to-one relationship with the `Department` entity. The `@JoinColumn` annotation is used to specify the mapping of the foreign key column `department_id`, which references the primary key of the `Department` table.

Now, let's write a sample program to demonstrate how to persist an `Employee` object along with its associated `Department`:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new department
        Department department = new Department();
        department.setName("Engineering");

        // Create a new employee
        Employee employee = new Employee();
        employee.setName("John Doe");
        employee.setDepartment(department);

        // Save the department and employee
        try (Session session = HibernateUtil.getSessionFactory().openSession()) {
            Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();
            session.save(department);
            session.save(employee);
            transaction.commit();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Retrieve the employee with its associated department
        try (Session session = HibernateUtil.getSessionFactory().openSession()) {
            Employee retrievedEmployee = session.get(Employee.class, employee.getId());
            System.out.println("Employee Name: " + retrievedEmployee.getName());
