You can achieve downloading JSON data using built-in browser features by creating a blob and utilizing the `URL.createObjectURL()` function. Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
import React from 'react';

const handleDownloadJSON = (jsonData) => {
  const jsonBlob = new Blob([JSON.stringify(jsonData, null, 2)], { type: 'application/json' });
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(jsonBlob);
  const link = document.createElement('a');
  link.href = url;
  link.setAttribute('download', 'data.json');
  document.body.appendChild(link);
  link.click();
};

const DownloadJSONButton = ({ jsonData }) => (
  <button onClick={() => handleDownloadJSON(jsonData)}>Download JSON</button>
);

export default DownloadJSONButton;
```

In this code:
- `handleDownloadJSON` function accepts JSON data as an argument, creates a blob from the JSON data, and creates a URL for the blob using `URL.createObjectURL()`.
- It then creates an anchor element (`<a>`) with the download attribute set to the desired file name (`data.json` in this case) and appends it to the document body.
- Finally, it triggers a click event on the anchor element, initiating the download process.

You can use this `DownloadJSONButton` component by passing your JSON data as a prop. When the button is clicked, it will trigger the download of the JSON data as a file named `data.json`.

