To open a URL stored in a JavaScript variable in a new tab when a span with text "view source" is clicked, you can use the `window.open` method in an event listener. Here’s how you can do it:

1. Define your HTML structure with a `<span>` element for the "view source" text.
2. Use JavaScript to add a click event listener to the `<span>` that opens the URL in a new tab.

Here’s an example:

**index.ejs** (or any HTML file)
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Open URL in New Tab</title>
</head>
<body>
    <span id="viewSourceSpan">view source</span>

    <script>
        // Store the URL in a variable
        const myurl = 'https://example.com';

        // Get the span by its ID
        const viewSourceSpan = document.getElementById('viewSourceSpan');

        // Add a click event listener to the span
        viewSourceSpan.addEventListener('click', function() {
            // Open the URL in a new tab
            window.open(myurl, '_blank');
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example:
1. The `<span>` with `id="viewSourceSpan"` is used to display the "view source" text.
2. The `myurl` variable stores the URL you want to open.
3. An event listener is added to the `<span>`. When the span is clicked, the `window.open` method is called with the URL and `_blank` as arguments, which opens the URL in a new tab.

