Yes, you can set default values for the `createdDate` and `updatedDate` fields in your Mongoose schema. To set default values, you can use the `default` option in the schema definition. Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

const taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  uniqueId: String,
  title: String,
  description: String,
  createdDate: {
    type: Date,
    default: Date.now, // Set the default value to the current date and time
  },
  updatedDate: {
    type: Date,
    default: Date.now, // Set the default value to the current date and time
  },
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('Task', taskSchema);
```

In the code above:

- We've specified the `createdDate` and `updatedDate` fields with a `type` of `Date`.

- We've used the `default` option to set the default value for both fields to the current date and time using `Date.now`.

With these changes, when you create a new task without explicitly specifying the `createdDate` and `updatedDate`, Mongoose will automatically set their values to the current date and time. For example:

```javascript
const newTask = new Task({
  uniqueId: 'task-001',
  title: 'Task 1',
  description: 'This is the description for Task 1',
});

newTask.save(); // The createdDate and updatedDate will be set to the current date and time
```

When you update a task, the `updatedDate` field will also be automatically updated to the current date and time.

