If you want to keep both the `id` field with BSON data and the `_id` field in your MongoDB documents, you can do so by adding a separate `id` field while keeping the `_id` field generated by MongoDB. Here's how you can modify your Mongoose schema to achieve this:

```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid');

const taskSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  id: {
    type: String,
    default: uuidv4,
    unique: true,
  },
  title: String,
  description: String,
  createdDate: {
    type: Date,
    default: Date.now,
  },
  updatedDate: {
    type: Date,
    default: Date.now,
  },
});

// Middleware to update the 'updatedDate' field before each 'update' operation
taskSchema.pre('update', function (next) {
  this.updatedDate = new Date();
  next();
});

// Create a virtual field to map 'id' to '_id' for backward compatibility
taskSchema.virtual('_id').get(function () {
  return this.id;
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('Task', taskSchema);
```

In this code:

- We've added a `virtual` field named `_id` using Mongoose's virtuals feature. This virtual field maps the `id` field to the `_id` field in the document.

- The `id` field will continue to hold the UUID generated by your application, and the `_id` field will hold the BSON ObjectId generated by MongoDB.

This way, you can use both `id` and `_id` in your documents for different purposes, and `_id` will still be present in the document for compatibility with MongoDB while keeping your application-level `id` field as well.

